/// DECLINE (2026-07-14, seed rcdzc): "borrowing op operand has an ownership this backend cannot yet
/// prove". `cdz check` is CLEAN; `cdz compile`/`cdz test` DECLINE at emit.
///
/// This GENERALIZES the earlier `decline-borrow-ownership-returned-map-string-eq` finding. That one
/// used `String ==` on the returned value; the essential trigger is NOT the `==` — it is:
///   (a) a value that ORIGINATES from a `Map.lookup` (a borrowed heap value out of the map),
///   (b) is RETURNED from a function (here wrapped in a `Out.Ok(t)` constructor), then
///   (c) is CONSUMED in the caller — a `match` on it is enough (no `==` needed).
/// Here the looked-up value is a COMPOUND (`Ty`), returned via a ctor, then `match`ed in `con`.
///
/// CONTROL (PASSES): if the caller consumes the returned value with a WILDCARD `Out.Ok(_)` (never
/// inspecting the borrowed payload), it compiles + runs — so it is specifically the borrowed value's
/// USE across the call boundary the backend can't prove, not the return itself.
/// CONTROL (PASSES): doing the `match` INSIDE the lookup arm (never returning the borrowed value) is
/// also fine.
///
/// IMPACT ON THE PORT: this is the fundamental shape of an environment lookup in a type-inference /
/// evaluation pass — look a binding up in a `Map String T` env, return it (or a Result wrapping it),
/// inspect it in the caller. `src/infer.cdz` hits it in its `Ref` arm (`Map.lookup(env, name)` →
/// `Outcome.Ok(t, s)`), so infer's `@test`s currently can't RUN (they `cdz check` clean). This is the
/// single biggest blocker to running HM inference end-to-end in the port.
///
/// SHARP BOUND (bisected 2026-07-14): the essential ingredient is a HEAP payload (here the `String`
/// inside `Ty.Con`) NESTED in the returned value. A `Ty` with ONLY SCALAR payloads (e.g.
/// `| Var(Int64) | Lvl(Int64)`), looked up from the map, returned via a ctor, and matched in the
/// caller, compiles + RUNS fine. So it is specifically a borrowed HEAP value (String/list/map/nested
/// sum) that escapes its lookup scope via a return that the backend can't prove owned. An explicit
/// `copy()` (deep re-construction) in the lookup arm does NOT sidestep it — the String payload is still
/// borrowed at the point it is read out.
///
/// Likely fix locus: the Perceus/borrow analysis in the backend — a heap value read out of a
/// persistent collection needs an owned (dup'd) handle when it escapes its lookup scope via a return.
type Ty = | Var(Int64) | Con(String)

type Out = | Ok(Ty) | Err(String)

def get(env: Map(String, Ty), name: String) -> Out = match Map.lookup(env, name) with
  | Option.Some(t) => Out.Ok(t)
  | Option.None(_) => Out.Err(name)

def con(o: Out) -> String = match o with
  | Out.Ok(t) => (match t with | Ty.Var(_) => "v" | Ty.Con(n) => n)
  | Out.Err(_) => "e"

@test
def lookup-return-match() =
  if con(get(Map.insert(#{}, "x", Ty.Con("Int")), "x")) == "Int" then unit else trap("x")

export { get }
